+  "instrumentation_gap_in_this_probe_a_timed_out_cell_loses_its_witnesses": {
+    "fix": "have the SIGTERM handler write the `pre` witness dict alongside the partial trace -- it is already fully populated by the time the action loop starts.",
+    "observed": "headb/sc25 was SIGTERM'd at the 1500s hard timeout, so its record has e3_dir_resolved=None and no witnesses -- visible in fairness_witnesses_per_cell, where that cell is absent.",
+    "what": "the cell's SIGTERM handler writes a MINIMAL record -- arm, game, seed, status, elapsed_s_at_kill, and the partial action trace. It does NOT write the fairness witnesses (e3_dir_resolved, effective sampler seed, imported module paths, observed model path / n_ctx, server exe and per-PID VRAM), because those live in the `pre` dict the handler does not close over.",
+    "why_it_does_not_change_this_verdict": "a truncated cell is excluded as a missing observation, so it contributes to neither the numerator nor the denominator of any rate here.",
+    "why_it_is_still_a_gap": "the TRUNCATION_ONLY classification itself is computed FROM that partial trace (the prefix comparison), so a claim is being made about a trace whose provenance the record cannot confirm -- we cannot verify from the artifact that the killed cell was running the arm it says it was, with the seed it says it used. Small here; it would matter if a truncated cell's prefix were ever load-bearing."
+  },
+  "instrumentation_shims_and_why_each_is_fair": {
+    "action_trace": "HEAD grew a native ProgressResult.action_trace (29daa97b0); the baseline has none -- the measuring instrument was itself part of the shipped work. Rather than backport it, the probe cell wraps E3AgentPolicy.next_move and records decisions itself: ONE instrument, defined outside both checkouts, applied identically to both arms, so a trace cannot differ because one arm records more carefully. HEAD's native trace is captured separately as a cross-check.",
+    "arm_config": "the baseline's ARM_CONFIGS has no frozen_gemma_pin entry and raised KeyError on the first launch attempt. The cell writes the config literal into ARM_CONFIGS on BOTH arms rather than falling back to `frozen` (which both define, byte-identically, but which runs under /no_think -- not the live gemma pin). Both apply_arm implementations were verified to read the same six keys.",
+    "policy_game_id": "HEAD's run_bounded_progress accepts policy_game_id; the baseline's does not. Passing it to HEAD alone would let the baseline's policy see the real game id and route on per-game prior knowledge while HEAD's could not -- a behavioural confound far larger than anything under test. The wrapper forces the anonymized id on both arms instead.",
+    "sampler_seed": "sampling_seed (added by 0da7f75ad, i.e. INSIDE the span under test) is patched into the base worktree from p2/seed_shim.patch. Verified: identical function body by AST comparison, and 4 call sites on each side. CONSEQUENCE, stated rather than buried: the A/B measures the 25-commit span MINUS the seed plumbing. That plumbing is default-OFF measurement infrastructure, not a behaviour change, so excluding it is the correct scoping -- but it IS an exclusion.",
+    "sampler_seed_asymmetry_found_and_closed": {
+      "method_note": "recorded because the CHECK is the transferable part: comparing call COUNTS passed and would have hidden this. Comparing (enclosing function, argument) pairs caught it.",
+      "what_i_found": "counting call sites (4 vs 4) was NOT sufficient. Comparing the ENCLOSING FUNCTION and the ARGUMENT of every `sampling_seed(...)` call shows the arms differ on one of the three seeded paths: HEAD calls `sampling_seed(attempt)` inside `_chat_complete_request`, while the shimmed baseline calls `sampling_seed(0)` there. So on retries (attempt>0) via the chat-template route the two arms would sample with DIFFERENT seeds -- exactly the confound the seeding exists to remove.",
+      "what_would_make_it_bite": "any future probe that enables `use_chat_template` (e.g. to run a Qwen or ThinkingCap generator, which need the assistant-turn structure) MUST NOT reuse this seed shim as-is: on retries the arms would diverge for seeding reasons rather than code reasons, and the divergence would look like a treatment effect. Verify the enclosing-function/argument pairs, not the call count.",
+      "why_it_is_INERT_for_this_probe": "both call sites are guarded by `if self.use_chat_template:`, and `use_chat_template: bool = False` is the dataclass default on BOTH arms (base line 3688, HEAD line 4141). The probe cell never sets it. So the chat-template route is not taken in any cell here, and the asymmetric call is unreachable. The two paths that ARE used -- `generate(attempt)` and `complete_text(0)` -- are identical on both arms.",
+      "why_the_shim_could_not_match_it": "base's `_chat_complete_request` has no `attempt` parameter at all; HEAD's signature is `(..., attempt: int = 0)`. That plumbing was added inside the span under test, so no patch to the baseline could replicate it without importing part of the treatment."
+    }
+  },
+  "known_limits_do_not_over_read": [
+    "the BASE-ARM noise floor covers only the 4 cells that carried the attributable finding, not all 12. Every other cell has NO base-side determinism witness, so its A/B classification rests on the head arm's floor alone -- the exact gap this replicate was added to close, closed only where it mattered most.",
+    "attributable-to-the-SPAN and attributable-to-INDUCTION are different numbers and the second is much smaller. Only a cell where a plan was found can evidence the induction-side work; a pre-induction explore reordering is a real behavioural difference but says nothing about what this session shipped. Cite the induction number when the claim is about the session's fixes.",
+    "the Fisher rows are NOT independent: the A/B arm is base-vs-head and the A/A arm is head-vs-headb, so the same 8 head runs appear on both sides of every table. Read the p-values as an effect-size sanity check, not as a formal test.",
+    "PYTHONHASHSEED is not pinned on either arm, so per-process str/bytes hash randomisation is a live nondeterminism channel that the seeding does not close. The A/A replicates MEASURE its consequences on the probed cells rather than eliminating it; a cell without an A/A replicate has that channel uncontrolled.",
+    "head is 2.5-3.1x slower than base on the two cells where the treatment demonstrably activates (vc33, tn36) while being faster on two others. Perturbing the agent is not the same as improving it, and the scored metric is efficiency-sensitive -- see wall_time_and_truncation_asymmetry.",
+    "budget=60 and max_inductions=1: a change that acts only at induction #2/#3, or only after a level-up deep in a 400-action run, is INVISIBLE here. An INERT verdict therefore means 'not detectably perturbing within 60 actions and 1 induction', NOT 'inert in all regimes'.",
+    "the A/B excludes the sampler-seed plumbing (see instrumentation_shims), because holding the sampler constant is what makes attribution possible at all.",
+    "a PERTURBS verdict would mean only that the treatment perturbs enough cells for the planned sign test to be ABLE to reach significance. Perturbation is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT for discordance on banked levels.",
+    "12 cells is ONE seed per game. A per-game seed replicate would separate game-specific from seed-specific perturbation; it was not run.",
+    "this is a PRE-FLIGHT, not a banked-levels measurement. It answers 'did the code reach the agent', never 'did the code help'.",
+    "see ab_divergence_locations: a perturbed cell whose first divergence lands before the ~24-transition explore budget is spent cannot have been caused by an induction-side change, because no world model has been proposed yet. Such a cell still counts as attributable perturbation (it IS a behavioural difference under a determinism-witnessed cell) but it does NOT evidence the induction-side fixes."
+  ],
