+    ],
+    "how_to_read_the_two_counts": "attributable_to_the_SPAN counts every cell where the A/B perturbed AND both arms were witnessed deterministic on that cell -- it answers 'did the 25-commit span change the live agent'. attributable_to_INDUCTION is the subset where a plan was found on at least one arm, i.e. where an induction-side change COULD have reached the actions -- it answers 'did the induction fixes change the live agent'. The second number is the one that speaks to what this session actually shipped, and it is much smaller. Reporting only the first would let explore-order reshuffling masquerade as the induction work paying off.",
+    "n_attributable_to_INDUCTION": 1,
+    "n_attributable_to_the_SPAN": 4,
+    "n_cells_with_no_plan_on_either_arm": 6,
+    "why_this_matters": "every commit in the span acts on INDUCTION, and an induction-side change can reach the ACTION STREAM only through a PLAN. On a cell where NEITHER arm found a plan the induction channel is closed, so INERT there means 'the induction gate rejected every candidate on both arms', i.e. the bottleneck is UPSTREAM of everything shipped, NOT 'the fixes are worthless' -- and PERTURBED there means the perturbation came from somewhere OTHER than induction. On a plan-found cell the channel was open, so INERT is the much stronger claim and PERTURBED is the only kind of evidence that speaks to the induction fixes."
